            {
              "id": "46eff04f-ea3a-31d9-98c7-b4aab4183b34",
              "name": "## Description\n\nOS command injection in Python process execution. Using os.system(command) passes a string to the system shell, so any untrusted data included in command can be interpreted as shell syntax instead of plain input.\n\nIn Python, this is dangerous because shell metacharacters such as ;, &&, |, $, and backticks can change which commands run. If command is influenced by user input, an attacker can execute arbitrary operating-system commands with the privileges of the running process.\n\nReplace shell-based execution with subprocess.run using an argument list and shell=False. Pass each command argument as a separate list element so Python invokes the program directly without shell parsing. If only a small set of commands is allowed, enforce an allowlist before execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not execute commands through os.system when any part of the command can be influenced by external input. Use subprocess.run or subprocess.Popen with shell=False and provide arguments as a list, not a single shell command string. Prefer direct library APIs instead of spawning system utilities whenever possible.\n\nIf command execution is required, restrict the executable and allowed arguments with a strict allowlist, validate input against expected formats, and run the process with the least privileges necessary. Avoid shell features such as pipes, redirection, variable expansion, and command chaining for untrusted data. If a shell cannot be avoided, quote each untrusted argument with shlex.quote as a secondary control, but treat that as weaker than removing the shell entirely.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\ndef run_command(command_args: list[str]) -> int:\n    allowed = {\"ls\", \"id\", \"whoami\"}\n    if not command_args or command_args[0] not in allowed:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid command\")\n\n    result = subprocess.run(command_args, check=False, shell=False)\n    return result.returncode\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess\ntyping\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\nimport os\nimport sys\n\n\ndef run_command(command: str) -> int:\n    # Vulnerable sink: untrusted input reaches a shell.\n    return os.system(command)\n\n\ndef build_command(user_input: str) -> str:\n    # Example: intended to ping a host, but concatenates raw input.\n    if os.name == \"nt\":\n        return f\"ping -n 1 {user_input}\"\n    return f\"ping -c 1 {user_input}\"\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    if len(sys.argv) < 2:\n        print(\"Usage: python app.py <target>\")\n        print('Example safe input: python app.py 127.0.0.1')\n        print('Example exploit:    python app.py \"127.0.0.1; whoami\"')\n        return 1\n\n    user_input = sys.argv[1]\n    command = build_command(user_input)\n\n    print(f\"[DEBUG] Executing: {command}\")\n    return run_command(command)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n```\n\n```text\n# requirements.txt\n# No external dependencies; standard library only.\n```\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"os-command-injection-demo\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.9\"\ndependencies = []\n```\n\n```bash\n# Run\npython app.py 127.0.0.1\n\n# Reproduce injection on Unix-like systems\npython app.py \"127.0.0.1; whoami\"\n\n# Reproduce injection on Windows\npython app.py \"127.0.0.1 & whoami\"\n```\n\n- Imports: `os`, `sys`\n- Dependencies: none beyond Python standard library"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-78",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html"
