            {
              "id": "48147daf-7c72-341e-856d-1d9adb126066",
              "name": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution in Python. Calling exec(code) executes the provided string as Python source, which allows an attacker to run arbitrary commands, read or modify data, and change application behavior if any part of that input is influenced by untrusted data.\n\nIn Python, exec is especially dangerous because it runs statements, not just expressions, and it executes with the current interpreter privileges unless tightly sandboxed. For this sink, the fix is to stop interpreting input as code. Replace exec with a safe alternative that maps allowed actions explicitly or parses only simple data formats when evaluation is actually needed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use exec on strings derived from users, files, environment variables, network responses, or other mutable sources. Model the required behavior explicitly with a whitelist of allowed commands, functions, or operations and dispatch through normal Python objects instead of generating code.\n\nIf the input represents data rather than behavior, parse it with a safe format such as json, or use ast.literal_eval only for trusted simple Python literal structures. If you must support configurable behavior, isolate it behind a constrained interface, validate inputs strictly, and avoid exposing builtins, imports, file access, or process execution to runtime-controlled strings.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom ast import literal_eval\n\nALLOWED_ACTIONS = {\n    \"start\": lambda: print(\"starting\"),\n    \"stop\": lambda: print(\"stopping\"),\n    \"status\": lambda: print(\"running\"),\n}\n\ndef run_action(action_name: str) -> None:\n    action = ALLOWED_ACTIONS.get(action_name)\n    if action is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"Unsupported action\")\n    action()\n\ndef parse_data(value: str):\n    return literal_eval(value)\n\n# Before:\n# exec(code)\n\n# After:\nrun_action(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom ast import literal_eval\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python Documentation: exec()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#exec)\n- [Python Documentation: ast.literal_eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```python\n# vulnerable_app.py\nimport os\nimport sys\n\ndef banner() -> None:\n    print(\"Vulnerable demo: user-controlled input reaches exec(code)\")\n    print(\"Example payload:\")\n    print(\"__import__('os').system('echo vulnerable && whoami')\")\n\ndef load_code() -> str:\n    # Untrusted source: command-line argument or environment variable\n    if len(sys.argv) > 1:\n        return sys.argv[1]\n    return os.environ.get(\"APP_CODE\", \"print('no code supplied')\")\n\ndef run() -> None:\n    banner()\n    code = load_code()\n    print(f\"\\n[debug] executing: {code}\\n\")\n    exec(code)  # Vulnerable sink\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    run()\n```\n\n```text\n# requirements.txt\n# No third-party dependencies\n```\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"exec-code-injection-demo\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Reproducible vulnerable console app demonstrating exec(code)\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\ndependencies = []\n```\n\n### Reproduce\n\nRun with a benign payload:\n\n```bash\npython vulnerable_app.py \"print('hello from exec')\"\n```\n\nRun with a malicious payload:\n\n```bash\npython vulnerable_app.py \"__import__('os').system('echo vulnerable')\"\n```\n\nOr via environment variable:\n\n```bash\nAPP_CODE=\"__import__('os').system('echo env-triggered')\" python vulnerable_app.py\n```\n\n### Why this is vulnerable\n\n`load_code()` reads attacker-controlled input from `sys.argv` or `APP_CODE`, then passes it directly to:\n\n```python\nexec(code)\n```\n\nThis executes arbitrary Python statements with the current process privileges."
