            {
              "id": "644b8010-960a-36da-9ca3-b6c5fda7a36a",
              "name": "## Description\n\nVulnerability label: insecure parsing of untrusted XML.\n\nIn Python, parsing attacker-controlled XML with xml.etree.ElementTree.fromstring can expose the application to XML-based denial-of-service conditions and related parser abuse. Even when external entities are not the primary concern, standard XML parsers can still consume excessive CPU or memory when given crafted input.\n\nThe sink shown here, ET.fromstring(xml), should not be used directly on untrusted data. Replace it with the hardened parser provided by defusedxml so the XML is parsed with security protections enabled by default. This keeps the code behavior similar while blocking dangerous XML constructs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse defusedxml for any XML that comes from requests, files, message queues, or other untrusted sources. Prefer importing defusedxml.ElementTree instead of xml.etree.ElementTree in modules that parse external XML. If changing imports globally is not practical, ensure the standard-library XML modules are hardened early in process startup with defusedxml.defuse_stdlib(), then keep XML size limits and input validation in place to reduce denial-of-service risk. Avoid enabling DTD processing or entity expansion for untrusted documents, and treat all XML input as hostile by default.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom defusedxml import ElementTree as ET\n\nnode = ET.fromstring(xml)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\npip install defusedxml\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [defusedxml on PyPI](https://pypi.org/project/defusedxml/)\n- [Python documentation: XML vulnerabilities](https://docs.python.org/3/library/xml.html#xml-vulnerabilities)\n- [OWASP XML Security Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/XML_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-611: Improper Restriction of XML External Entity Reference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/611.html)\n",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```python\n# vulnerable_xml_app.py\nimport sys\nimport xml.etree.ElementTree as ET\n\n\ndef read_xml() -> str:\n    if len(sys.argv) > 1:\n        with open(sys.argv[1], \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            return f.read()\n    return sys.stdin.read()\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    xml = read_xml()  # Untrusted input from file or stdin\n\n    # Vulnerable sink: insecure parsing of untrusted XML\n    node = ET.fromstring(xml)\n\n    print(f\"Root tag: {node.tag}\")\n    print(f\"Children: {len(list(node))}\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n```text\n# requirements.txt\n# No third-party dependencies for the vulnerable example\n```\n\n```bash\n# Run with stdin\npython vulnerable_xml_app.py <<'EOF'\n<root><item>Hello</item></root>\nEOF\n\n# Or run with a file\npython vulnerable_xml_app.py input.xml\n```\n\n```xml\n<!-- example input: input.xml -->\n<?xml version=\"1.0\"?>\n<!DOCTYPE lolz [\n <!ENTITY a \"1234567890\">\n <!ENTITY b \"&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;\">\n <!ENTITY c \"&b;&b;&b;&b;&b;&b;&b;&b;&b;&b;\">\n]>\n<root>&c;</root>\n```\n\n```python\n# Safe replacement for the sink\nfrom defusedxml import ElementTree as ET\n\nnode = ET.fromstring(xml)\n```\n\n```text\n# requirements.txt for the fixed version\ndefusedxml>=0.7.1\n```"
