            {
              "id": "65d8f510-285e-3c34-b079-c11a947d763c",
              "name": "## Description\n\nOS command injection in process creation. In Python, calling subprocess.Popen with shell=True causes the command string to be interpreted by the system shell, so any untrusted input concatenated into that command can change its meaning and execute unintended commands.\n\nFor this sink, the fix is to avoid the shell entirely and pass the executable and arguments as a list to subprocess.Popen with shell=False. This prevents shell metacharacters from being parsed. If external input influences arguments, validate it against a strict allowlist and reject unexpected values before invoking the process.\n\nWhen shell features are truly required, redesign the call so user input is never embedded in the shell command. Prefer direct invocation of the target program, use absolute paths for trusted executables, and keep capture and error handling explicit so the process behavior remains predictable.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not build shell command strings from variables or request data. Use subprocess.Popen, subprocess.run, or related APIs with a sequence of arguments and keep shell=False. Validate user-controlled values with an allowlist of accepted commands, flags, or filenames, and map user choices to fixed argument sets instead of passing raw input through. Use absolute executable paths, least-privilege execution, and avoid inheriting unnecessary environment variables or working-directory state when starting child processes.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\nuser_arg = get_validated_argument()\n\nprocess = subprocess.Popen(\n    [\"/usr/bin/mycommand\", user_arg],\n    shell=False,\n    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n    stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n    text=True,\n)\nstdout, stderr = process.communicate()\n\nif process.returncode != 0:\n    raise subprocess.CalledProcessError(process.returncode, process.args, output=stdout, stderr=stderr)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nsubprocess (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [OWASP OS Command Injection Defense Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OS_Command_Injection_Defense_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```python\nimport argparse\nimport subprocess\n\n\ndef run():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"--name\",\n        required=True,\n        help=\"Untrusted input that is concatenated into a shell command\",\n    )\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n\n    # Vulnerable sink: user input reaches subprocess.Popen with shell=True\n    cmd = \"echo Hello \" + args.name\n    process = subprocess.Popen(\n        cmd,\n        shell=True,\n        stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n        stderr=subprocess.PIPE,\n        text=True,\n    )\n    stdout, stderr = process.communicate()\n\n    print(\"Command:\", cmd)\n    print(\"Return code:\", process.returncode)\n    print(\"STDOUT:\")\n    print(stdout)\n    print(\"STDERR:\")\n    print(stderr)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    run()\n```\n\nExample usage:\n\n```bash\npython app.py --name world\npython app.py --name \"world; whoami\"\n```\n\n`requirements.txt`\n```txt\n# No external dependencies; Python standard library only\n```\n\n`pyproject.toml`\n```toml\n[project]\nname = \"popen-shell-true-demo\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\ndependencies = []\n```\n\nNotes:\n- This is intentionally vulnerable for reproduction.\n- The specific sink is:\n\n```python\nprocess = subprocess.Popen(\n```\n\n- The issue is triggered because `shell=True` causes shell metacharacters in `args.name` to be interpreted."
              },
