            {
              "id": "6daea7a1-439f-3307-95f6-1c0ab0164eb3",
              "name": "## Description\n\nThis is use of a weak cryptographic hash algorithm. In Python, hashlib.md5() creates an MD5 digest, which is no longer appropriate for security-sensitive purposes such as password hashing, digital signatures, or integrity checks against malicious tampering.\n\nMD5 is vulnerable to collision attacks, so an attacker can create different inputs that produce the same digest. For the sink shown here, replace hashlib.md5() with a stronger primitive based on the actual purpose: use hashlib.sha256() or stronger for general hashing and integrity uses, and use a password hashing function such as hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac(), bcrypt, or Argon2 for passwords.\n\nIf the digest is used to protect authenticity, use a keyed construction such as hmac with SHA-256 instead of a plain hash. If it is only for non-security purposes such as cache keys or duplicate detection, document that intent clearly so weak algorithms are not reused in a security context later.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use MD5 or SHA-1 for any security decision. For general-purpose cryptographic hashing in Python, prefer hashlib.sha256(), hashlib.sha384(), or hashlib.sha512(). For password storage, use a dedicated password-KDF with a unique salt and a work factor, such as hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac() from the standard library or a modern library such as Argon2 or bcrypt. For message authenticity, use hmac.new(key, data, hashlib.sha256) rather than a raw hash. Review any stored digests or protocol fields derived from MD5 and migrate them to stronger algorithms in a backward-compatible way where necessary.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\ndata = b\"example payload\"\n\nh = hashlib.sha256()\nh.update(data)\ndigest = h.hexdigest()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib\nhmac\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Use_of_a_Broken_or_Risky_Cryptographic_Algorithm)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```python\n# app.py\nimport hashlib\nimport sys\n\n\ndef insecure_file_digest(path: str) -> str:\n    # Vulnerable sink: weak hash algorithm for integrity/security usage\n    h = hashlib.md5()\n\n    with open(path, \"rb\") as f:\n        for chunk in iter(lambda: f.read(8192), b\"\"):\n            h.update(chunk)\n\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    if len(sys.argv) != 2:\n        print(f\"Usage: python {sys.argv[0]} <file>\")\n        return 1\n\n    target = sys.argv[1]\n    try:\n        digest = insecure_file_digest(target)\n        print(f\"MD5({target}) = {digest}\")\n        print(\"WARNING: This uses hashlib.md5() and is intentionally vulnerable.\")\n        return 0\n    except FileNotFoundError:\n        print(f\"File not found: {target}\")\n        return 2\n    except OSError as exc:\n        print(f\"I/O error: {exc}\")\n        return 3\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n```\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"md5-vuln-demo\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Reproducible Python console app demonstrating insecure hashlib.md5() usage\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\ndependencies = []\n```\n\n```text\n# requirements.txt\n# No external dependencies; uses only Python standard library.\n```\n\n### Run\n```bash\npython app.py sample.txt\n```\n\n### Notes\n- This example intentionally targets the sink:\n  ```python\n  h = hashlib.md5()\n  ```\n- It demonstrates insecure use of MD5 for file integrity-style hashing in a console application.\n- Standard library imports used: `hashlib`, `sys`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
