            {
              "id": "879ada17-1f55-367f-9351-21fdb24acb7e",
              "name": "## Description\n\nUnsafe dynamic module loading. In Python, passing untrusted data to importlib.import_module() allows an attacker to influence which module is imported and executed, which can result in unauthorized code execution or loading of unintended application components.\n\nThe sink shown, return import_module(module_name), is risky because module_name can select any importable module visible to the runtime. The fix is to stop importing arbitrary names and instead map a small set of allowed identifiers to known-safe module paths. Validate the input against that allowlist before calling import_module(), and reject anything that is not explicitly permitted.\n\nThis approach preserves dynamic behavior where needed while ensuring imports are limited to expected modules only. It also makes the permitted plugin or handler surface explicit, easier to review, and safer to maintain.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not pass user-controlled strings directly to importlib.import_module(). Replace free-form module names with stable application identifiers and resolve them through a hardcoded allowlist. Keep the allowlist small, reviewable, and limited to trusted modules under your control. Reject unknown values explicitly instead of attempting to sanitize arbitrary import paths.\n\nIf extensibility is required, load plugins from a trusted registry defined in code or configuration that is writable only by administrators. Avoid importing from request parameters, headers, query strings, form fields, or other external input. Log rejected import requests for monitoring, and ensure imported modules do not have unsafe side effects at import time.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom importlib import import_module\n\n_ALLOWED_MODULES = {\n    \"json_handler\": \"Package.safe_modules.json_handler\",\n    \"xml_handler\": \"Package.safe_modules.xml_handler\",\n}\n\n\ndef load_module(module_name: str):\n    try:\n        safe_module_path = _ALLOWED_MODULES[module_name]\n    except KeyError:\n        raise ValueError(\"Unsupported module name\")\n\n    return import_module(safe_module_path)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom importlib import import_module\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Unsafe Use of Reflection](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Unsafe_use_of_Reflection)\n- [CWE-470: Use of Externally-Controlled Input to Select Classes or Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/470.html)\n- [Python Documentation: importlib.import_module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/importlib.html#importlib.import_module)\n- [OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```python\n# app.py\nimport argparse\nfrom importlib import import_module\n\n\ndef load_module(module_name: str):\n    # Vulnerable sink: user-controlled input reaches import_module()\n    return import_module(module_name)\n\n\ndef main():\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()\n    parser.add_argument(\n        \"module_name\",\n        help=\"Module to import, e.g. json or malicious_plugin\",\n    )\n    args = parser.parse_args()\n\n    module = load_module(args.module_name)\n    print(f\"Imported: {module.__name__}\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n```python\n# malicious_plugin.py\nprint(\"malicious_plugin imported: attacker-controlled code executed at import time\")\n\nVALUE = \"owned\"\n```\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"unsafe-dynamic-import-demo\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Reproducible example of unsafe importlib.import_module usage\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\ndependencies = []\n```\n\n**How to run**\n\n```bash\npython app.py json\npython app.py malicious_plugin\n```\n\n**Why this reproduces the issue**\n\n`app.py` accepts `module_name` from the console and passes it directly to:\n\n```python\nreturn import_module(module_name)\n```\n\nBecause the value is untrusted, an attacker can choose any importable module visible to Python, including a local module like `malicious_plugin.py`, whose top-level code runs immediately on import."
