              "id": "971fb3d3-2e80-39f1-a029-be7c5b5ed5ef",
              "name": "## Description\n\nUnsafe YAML deserialization is present here. In Python, calling yaml.load with yaml.UnsafeLoader allows specially crafted YAML content to construct arbitrary Python objects during parsing, which can lead to code execution when the input is attacker-controlled.\n\nThe sink shown returns yaml.load(user_input, Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader), so untrusted YAML is being deserialized with an explicitly unsafe loader. Replace it with yaml.safe_load or yaml.load(..., Loader=yaml.SafeLoader) so the parser only creates standard data types such as dict, list, string, and number values.\n\nIf the application must accept YAML from external users, also validate the parsed structure against the expected schema before using it. Treat all YAML input as untrusted, reject unexpected keys or nested types, and avoid any loader that can instantiate Python-specific objects.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse yaml.safe_load for any YAML derived from users, requests, files, environment variables, or other untrusted sources. Do not use yaml.UnsafeLoader, yaml.Loader, yaml.CLoader, or yaml.unsafe_load on attacker-controlled input. After parsing, validate that the result matches the expected shape and types before passing it deeper into the application. If only a small fixed structure is allowed, enforce required keys, reject unknown fields, and fail closed on malformed YAML.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport yaml\n\ndef parse_user_yaml(user_input: str):\n    return yaml.safe_load(user_input)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nPyYAML\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Unsafe Deserialization](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Unsafe_Deserialization)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [PyYAML Documentation](https://pyyaml.org/wiki/PyYAMLDocumentation)\n- [PyYAML unsafe_load remote code execution overview](https://sourcery.ai/vulnerabilities/python-pyyaml-unsafe-load-rce)\n",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```python\n# app.py\nimport sys\nimport yaml\n\n\ndef parse_user_yaml(user_input: str):\n    # Vulnerable sink: attacker-controlled YAML reaches UnsafeLoader\n    return yaml.load(user_input, Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader)\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    if len(sys.argv) > 1:\n        with open(sys.argv[1], \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            user_input = f.read()\n    else:\n        print(\"Paste YAML, then press Ctrl-D (Linux/macOS) or Ctrl-Z Enter (Windows):\")\n        user_input = sys.stdin.read()\n\n    try:\n        result = parse_user_yaml(user_input)\n        print(\"Parsed object:\", result)\n    except Exception as exc:\n        print(f\"Error: {exc}\")\n        return 1\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n```\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"pyyaml-unsafe-loader-demo\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.9\"\ndependencies = [\n  \"PyYAML>=6.0\",\n]\n```\n\n```yaml\n# payload.yml\n!!python/object/apply:os.system [\"echo vulnerable > /tmp/unsafe_yaml_demo\"]\n```\n\n```bash\npython app.py payload.yml\n```\n\nExpected effect on Unix-like systems: the YAML parser invokes `os.system(...)` during deserialization because `yaml.UnsafeLoader` permits Python object construction.\n\nSafer replacement:\n\n```python\nimport yaml\n\ndef parse_user_yaml(user_input: str):\n    return yaml.safe_load(user_input)\n```"
