              "id": "c5b49cf2-f7b0-376e-a2ff-97265467e8c3",
              "name": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm. This code creates a SHA-1 hash object with hashlib.sha1(), which is not appropriate for security-sensitive purposes such as integrity protection, digital signatures, password handling, or token generation because SHA-1 is vulnerable to collision attacks.\n\nIn Python, replace SHA-1 with a stronger hash such as SHA-256 or SHA-512 when the value is used for security decisions. If the hash is only needed for a non-security purpose such as deduplication or compatibility with a legacy format, make that intent explicit by passing usedforsecurity=False where supported. For the sink shown here, the preferred fix is to switch to hashlib.sha256() unless there is a documented non-security requirement.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse SHA-256 or stronger algorithms for security-relevant hashing in Python. For passwords, do not use general-purpose hashes directly; use a dedicated password hashing function such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2. If legacy code must keep SHA-1 for non-security uses, call hashlib.sha1(usedforsecurity=False) when the runtime supports it and document that the digest is not used to enforce security properties. Review all consumers of the hash to ensure the value is not relied on for authentication, signature verification, integrity enforcement, or uniqueness in adversarial contexts.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport hashlib\n\ndata = b\"example data\"\nh = hashlib.sha256()\nh.update(data)\ndigest = h.hexdigest()\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nhashlib (Python standard library)\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Secure Hash Standard (FIPS 180-4)](https://www.nist.gov/publications/secure-hash-standard-shs-fips-180-4)\n",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```python\n# vulnerable_sha1_app.py\nimport hashlib\nimport sys\n\n\ndef generate_token(username: str, secret: str) -> str:\n    # Vulnerable sink: SHA-1 used for a security-sensitive token\n    h = hashlib.sha1()\n    h.update(f\"{username}:{secret}\".encode(\"utf-8\"))\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    if len(sys.argv) != 3:\n        print(\"Usage: python vulnerable_sha1_app.py <username> <secret>\")\n        return 1\n\n    username = sys.argv[1]\n    secret = sys.argv[2]\n\n    token = generate_token(username, secret)\n    print(f\"Generated token for {username}: {token}\")\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main())\n```\n\n```text\n# requirements.txt\n# No external dependencies; uses Python standard library only.\n```\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"vulnerable-sha1-app\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Reproducible example using hashlib.sha1() in a security-sensitive context\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\ndependencies = []\n```\n\n```bash\npython vulnerable_sha1_app.py alice supersecret\n```\n\nThis reproduces the specific sink:\n\n```python\nh = hashlib.sha1()\n```\n\nIt is intentionally vulnerable because the SHA-1 digest is used as a security-relevant token."
