              "id": "d5153ce7-eb32-3e5a-aaa1-b6a2fa505ca3",
              "name": "## Description\n\nWeak cryptographic hash algorithm usage. This code computes an MD5 digest from user-controlled input and converts it to hexadecimal text. In Python, hashlib.md5 remains available for legacy compatibility, but MD5 is not suitable for security-sensitive purposes because collision attacks are practical.\n\nIf this digest is used for integrity checks, security decisions, signatures, token generation, or password-related processing, an attacker can exploit MD5's weaknesses to produce different inputs with the same hash. Replace MD5 with a modern hash such as SHA-256 for general-purpose security hashing. If the operation is explicitly non-security-related, use hashlib.md5(..., usedforsecurity=False) to document that intent and satisfy restricted runtimes.\n\nFor the sink shown, the direct fix is to switch to hashlib.sha256 and ensure the input is bytes before hashing. This preserves the same hex-encoded output pattern while using a stronger algorithm.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nUse hashlib.sha256 or a stronger algorithm for security-relevant hashing in Python. Normalize string input to bytes explicitly with UTF-8 before hashing so behavior is predictable across callers. Keep the hexadecimal encoding step only if downstream code expects text output.\n\nIf the hash is intended for password storage, do not use a plain fast hash at all; use hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac, bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 with a unique salt and appropriate work factor. If the code is truly for a non-security purpose such as deduplication of trusted data, mark that intent explicitly with hashlib.md5(data, usedforsecurity=False) rather than relying on MD5 in a security context.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom binascii import hexlify\nfrom hashlib import sha256\n\nif isinstance(user_input, str):\n    user_input = user_input.encode(\"utf-8\")\n\ndigest = hexlify(sha256(user_input).digest()).decode(\"utf-8\")\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom binascii import hexlify\nfrom hashlib import sha256\n\nPassword hashing alternatives if needed:\npip install argon2-cffi\npip install bcrypt\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Cryptographic Storage Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cryptographic_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-327: Use of a Broken or Risky Cryptographic Algorithm](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/327.html)\n- [Python hashlib documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/hashlib.html)\n- [RFC 6151: Updated Security Considerations for the MD5 Message-Digest and the HMAC-MD5 Algorithms](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6151)\n",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```python\n# vulnerable_md5_console.py\nfrom binascii import hexlify\nfrom hashlib import md5\nimport sys\n\n\ndef to_bytes(user_input):\n    if isinstance(user_input, str):\n        return user_input.encode(\"utf-8\")\n    return user_input\n\n\ndef main():\n    if len(sys.argv) < 2:\n        print(f\"Usage: {sys.argv[0]} <user_input>\")\n        sys.exit(1)\n\n    user_input = sys.argv[1]\n    user_input = to_bytes(user_input)\n\n    # Vulnerable sink: weak cryptographic hash on user-controlled input\n    digest = hexlify(md5(user_input).digest()).decode(\"utf8\")\n\n    print(\"Input:\", sys.argv[1])\n    print(\"MD5 hex digest:\", digest)\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n```text\n# requirements.txt\n# No external dependencies; Python standard library only.\n```\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"vulnerable-md5-console\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Reproducible example of weak MD5 hashing on user-controlled input\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\ndependencies = []\n```\n\n```bash\npython vulnerable_md5_console.py \"attacker-controlled-data\"\n```\n\n```python\n# vulnerable pattern reproduced exactly\ndigest = hexlify(md5(user_input).digest()).decode(\"utf8\")\n```"
