            {
              "id": "e4e927eb-c261-3472-8803-e184891e5b9a",
              "name": "## Description\n\nUnsafe YAML deserialization. In Python, using PyYAML with `yaml.load_all(..., Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader)` allows YAML documents to instantiate arbitrary Python objects during parsing. If an attacker can influence `user_input`, they can supply crafted YAML that triggers code execution or other dangerous side effects when deserialized.\n\nFor the sink shown, the fix is to replace the unsafe loader with `yaml.safe_load_all` or `yaml.load_all(..., Loader=yaml.SafeLoader)`. That limits parsing to standard YAML types such as strings, numbers, lists, and dictionaries, which prevents execution-oriented object construction from untrusted input.\n\nIf the application genuinely requires custom Python objects, do not deserialize directly from untrusted sources. Define a strict schema for the expected document structure, validate fields after parsing, and map validated data into application objects explicitly instead of letting the YAML parser create them automatically.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use `yaml.unsafe_load`, `yaml.UnsafeLoader`, `yaml.Loader`, or `yaml.CLoader` for data that can come from users, files, network requests, message queues, or any other untrusted source. Parse YAML with `yaml.safe_load` or `yaml.safe_load_all` when multiple documents are expected, and treat the parsed result as plain data only.\n\nAfter safe parsing, validate the structure and allowed values before using the data. Restrict expected keys, types, lengths, and ranges, and reject documents that contain unexpected content. If advanced object deserialization is required for trusted internal data, isolate that behavior from untrusted inputs and prefer explicit construction of objects from validated dictionaries.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport yaml\n\nresult = list(yaml.safe_load_all(user_input))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nPyYAML\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [PyYAML Documentation](https://pyyaml.org/wiki/PyYAMLDocumentation)\n- [PyYAML unsafe_load remote code execution overview](https://sourcery.ai/vulnerabilities/python-pyyaml-unsafe-load-rce)\n",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```python\n# vulnerable_app.py\nimport os\nimport yaml\n\ndef run(user_input: str) -> None:\n    print(\"[*] Parsing YAML with UnsafeLoader...\")\n    result = yaml.load_all(user_input, Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader)  # vulnerable sink\n    docs = list(result)\n    print(\"[*] Parsed documents:\", docs)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    payload = \"\"\"!!python/object/apply:os.system\n- \"echo vulnerable > pyyaml_poc.txt\"\n---\n{\"status\": \"second document parsed too\"}\n\"\"\"\n    print(\"[*] Payload:\")\n    print(payload)\n    run(payload)\n    print(\"[*] Check created file:\", os.path.abspath(\"pyyaml_poc.txt\"))\n```\n\n```text\n# requirements.txt\nPyYAML>=6.0\n```\n\n### How to run\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate  # Windows: .venv\\Scripts\\activate\npip install -r requirements.txt\npython vulnerable_app.py\ncat pyyaml_poc.txt  # Windows: type pyyaml_poc.txt\n```\n\n### Why this reproduces the issue\n\nThe first YAML document uses a PyYAML Python-specific tag:\n\n```yaml\n!!python/object/apply:os.system\n- \"echo vulnerable > pyyaml_poc.txt\"\n```\n\nBecause the code calls:\n\n```python\nresult = yaml.load_all(user_input, Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader)\n```\n\nPyYAML permits arbitrary object/function construction and invocation during deserialization. Here it invokes `os.system(...)`, demonstrating dangerous side effects from attacker-controlled input.\n\n### Safe version\n\n```python\nimport yaml\n\ndef run_safe(user_input: str) -> None:\n    result = list(yaml.safe_load_all(user_input))\n    print(result)\n```\n\nWith `safe_load_all`, the malicious tag is rejected instead of executed."
