        {
          "ruleId": "fd5efed1-3906-377f-8293-1683f694011e",
          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "## Description\n\nThis finding is an OS command injection risk. In Python, calling os.system(cmd) passes the string to the system shell, so any untrusted data embedded in cmd can change the command being executed by adding shell metacharacters, command separators, redirections, or substitutions.\n\nThe safest fix is to avoid the shell entirely and execute the intended program with subprocess.run using a list of arguments and shell=False. This prevents the shell from interpreting attacker-controlled input as additional commands. If the command includes user input, validate it against a strict allowlist of expected values before passing it to the process.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace os.system with subprocess.run, subprocess.Popen, or similar APIs invoked with shell=False and a list of arguments rather than a single command string. Do not build shell command lines through string concatenation, formatting, or f-strings when any part may come from external input.\n\nConstrain executable paths and arguments to known-safe values. Prefer fixed command paths, strict allowlists, and type or range validation for user-supplied parameters. Only if shell execution is absolutely unavoidable should values be quoted with shlex.quote, but avoiding the shell is the primary mitigation because quoting mistakes are easy and platform-specific.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\n\n\ndef run_command(user_arg: str) -> None:\n    allowed_values = {\"start\", \"stop\", \"status\"}\n    if user_arg not in allowed_values:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid command argument\")\n\n    subprocess.run([\"/usr/bin/mytool\", user_arg], check=True, shell=False)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\nimport shlex\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Python shlex module documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/shlex.html)\n"
          },
