        {
          "ruleId": "393820c2-e77a-3b0a-babb-393ca29206ea",
          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "## Description\n\nThis issue is missing function-level access control. In Python web handlers, a route that reads request data and performs application logic without first enforcing authentication and authorization can be invoked by any caller who can reach the endpoint.\n\nThe sink shown reads a query parameter directly from the incoming aiohttp request. If the surrounding view or handler is not protected, an unauthenticated or unauthorized user can supply input and trigger behavior that should be limited to approved users. The fix is to enforce authentication before processing the request and to check authorization for the specific action or resource, returning 401 or 403 instead of continuing.\n\nIn this context, apply the access-control check at the handler entry point, not inside the helper alone. Keep the helper focused on parsing input, but ensure the route only calls it after the user identity has been established and permissions have been verified.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication and authorization on every route that exposes protected behavior. In Python web applications, perform the check at the start of the request handler or through framework middleware or decorators so the endpoint cannot be reached without an established identity. Deny by default, return 401 when no valid identity is present, and return 403 when the user lacks the required permission.\n\nDo not rely on a helper that only parses request parameters to provide security. Keep input parsing separate from access control, and ensure authorization is tied to the specific action or resource being accessed. Prefer centralized checks, consistent permission names, and server-side identity data derived from a validated session or token rather than request parameters.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom aiohttp import web\n\n\ndef _get_user_input(request):\n    return request.rel_url.query.get(\"user_input\", \"\")\n\n\ndef get_authenticated_user(request):\n    user = request.get(\"user\")\n    if user is None:\n        raise web.HTTPUnauthorized(text=\"Authentication required\")\n    return user\n\n\ndef require_permission(user, permission):\n    permissions = user.get(\"permissions\", set())\n    if permission not in permissions:\n        raise web.HTTPForbidden(text=\"Insufficient permissions\")\n\n\nasync def protected_view(request):\n    user = get_authenticated_user(request)\n    require_permission(user, \"read:user_input\")\n\n    user_input = _get_user_input(request)\n    return web.json_response({\"user_input\": user_input})\n\n\n@web.middleware\nasync def auth_middleware(request, handler):\n    auth_header = request.headers.get(\"Authorization\", \"\")\n\n    if auth_header == \"Bearer trusted-token\":\n        request[\"user\"] = {\n            \"id\": \"123\",\n            \"permissions\": {\"read:user_input\"}\n        }\n\n    return await handler(request)\n\n\napp = web.Application(middlewares=[auth_middleware])\napp.router.add_get(\"/protected\", protected_view)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\naiohttp>=3.9.0\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [aiohttp Web Server Quickstart](https://docs.aiohttp.org/en/stable/web_quickstart.html)\n"
          },
