        {
          "ruleId": "4cd1467d-b799-3d52-a087-5378ededda28",
          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "## Description\n\nBroken function-level access control. This view returns a server-rendered template without enforcing authentication or permission checks, so any caller who can reach the route may be able to access functionality or content that should be restricted.\n\nIn Python web applications, access control should be enforced explicitly at the view entry point. For a Django-style function view like this one, add authentication and, when appropriate, a specific permission check before rendering the response. This ensures the endpoint is denied by default unless the user is logged in and authorized for the action.\n\nThe sink shown also formats a template name dynamically. Restricting access is the primary fix for this finding, but it is also safer to avoid rendering arbitrary template names derived from variables unless they are validated against an allowlist. Keep authorization checks close to the view and validate any user-influenced template selection separately.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication on every non-public view and enforce authorization with role- or permission-based checks at the function entry point. In Django, use login_required for authenticated access and permission_required, user_passes_test, or object-level authorization for restricted actions.\n\nDo not rely on routing conventions, hidden links, or client-side checks to protect endpoints. Deny access by default, return 403 for authenticated users without permission, and document which views are intentionally public.\n\nIf the rendered template name can be influenced by request data or other untrusted input, validate it against a fixed allowlist before calling get_template. This prevents users from reaching unintended templates even after access control is added.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.http import Http404, HttpResponse\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\n\nALLOWED_TEMPLATES = {\"home\", \"dashboard\", \"profile\"}\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"app.view_page\", raise_exception=True)\ndef _root(request, name):\n    if name not in ALLOWED_TEMPLATES:\n        raise Http404(\"Template not found\")\n\n    template = get_template(f\"{name}.html\")\n    return HttpResponse(template.render({}, request))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Top 10: Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-306: Missing Authentication for Critical Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/306.html)\n- [CWE-862: Missing Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/862.html)\n- [Django authentication and authorization](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [Django view decorators](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/http/decorators/)\n"
          },
