        {
          "ruleId": "5d7ffd57-ee5f-303e-aefa-f0ba35aaa3ea",
          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "## Description\n\nThis is a missing function-level access control issue. The view is directly invokable and performs sensitive behavior without first enforcing authentication and authorization for the current user.\n\nIn this Python web handler, the request reaches code that resolves a trigger, invokes a callable, loads a template by name, and returns a response before any access check is applied. That means an unauthenticated or unauthorized caller may be able to execute server-side actions or access functionality that should be restricted. Because the handler uses request-controlled values such as name and trigger to select behavior, the lack of a permission gate increases the impact.\n\nThe fix is to enforce access control at the entry point of the view and validate that the authenticated user is allowed to use the requested action. In Django, this is commonly done with authentication decorators for sync views, or with an explicit async check at the start of an async view. Follow the authentication check with a permission test tied to the specific operation being requested.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nRequire authentication before executing any view that exposes non-public functionality, and perform authorization checks inside the handler based on the current user and the requested action. For async Django views, use an explicit early return or an async-compatible wrapper so the check runs before any business logic.\n\nDo not rely on routing, obscurity, or client-provided parameters to decide who may invoke a function. Map each operation to a permission, role, or ownership rule, and deny by default when the user is unauthenticated or the requested action is not explicitly allowed. Keep sensitive callable dispatch behind an allowlist rather than passing untrusted values directly into resolver logic.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseForbidden\nfrom django.template.loader import get_template\nfrom asgiref.sync import sync_to_async\n\nALLOWED_TRIGGERS = {\n    \"safe\": {\"default\"},\n}\n\n@sync_to_async\ndef _is_authorized(user, name, trigger):\n    if not user.is_authenticated:\n        return False\n    if not user.has_perm(\"app.view_package_class\"):\n        return False\n    return trigger in ALLOWED_TRIGGERS.get(name, set())\n\nasync def _view(request):\n    user_input = await _get_user_input(request)\n    name = request.GET.get(\"name\", \"\")\n    trigger = request.GET.get(\"trigger\", \"\")\n\n    if not await _is_authorized(request.user, name, trigger):\n        return HttpResponseForbidden(\"Forbidden\")\n\n    trigger_func = get_trigger(name, trigger)\n    if trigger_func:\n        trigger_func(user_input)\n\n    template = get_template(f\"{name}.html\")\n    return HttpResponse(template.render({}, request))\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nDjango\nasgiref\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Broken Access Control](https://owasp.org/www-community/Broken_Access_Control)\n- [OWASP Authorization Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authorization_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Authentication Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Django authentication system](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/auth/default/)\n- [CWE-285: Improper Authorization](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/285.html)\n"
          },
