        {
          "ruleId": "47c44dac-29f9-3615-bc70-7019690c6759",
          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "## Description\n\nThis is an OS command injection risk in Python process execution. Calling subprocess.Popen with shell=True causes the command to be interpreted by the system shell, which allows shell metacharacters, environment expansion, pipes, redirects, and command chaining to affect execution.\n\nIn this case, the sink shown passes command to Popen while capturing stdout and stderr. If any part of that command can be influenced by external input, an attacker can execute unintended commands. The correct fix is to avoid the shell entirely, pass the program and arguments as a list, and keep shell=False so Python invokes the target process directly without shell parsing.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not use shell=True for normal command execution. Build the command as an argument list such as [\"program\", \"arg1\", \"arg2\"] and call subprocess.Popen, subprocess.run, or related APIs with shell=False. This prevents shell parsing of metacharacters and removes common command injection paths.\n\nPrefer fixed executable paths and allowlisted arguments when user input is involved. Validate input against expected formats before using it in process execution. If you must capture output, keep stdout and stderr configured as needed, but still invoke the process directly. Only use shell=True when shell features are strictly required, and in that case avoid passing untrusted input entirely.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\nimport shlex\n\ncommand_args = shlex.split(command) if isinstance(command, str) else command\n\nresult = subprocess.Popen(\n    command_args,\n    stdout=subprocess.PIPE,\n    stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,\n    shell=False,\n    text=True\n)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport subprocess\nimport shlex\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Command Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Command_Injection)\n- [CWE-78: Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\n- [Python subprocess documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html)\n- [Prevent Command Injection in Python subprocess usage](https://securecodingpractices.com/prevent-command-injection-python-subprocess/)\n"
          },
