        {
          "ruleId": "3ea7f8ca-952c-3710-bae1-de2c6d871862",
          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "## Description\n\nUnsafe YAML deserialization. In Python, calling yaml.load on untrusted input with yaml.UnsafeLoader allows YAML tags to construct arbitrary Python objects during parsing. If an attacker controls user_input, this can lead to unexpected object creation, code execution paths, or application compromise.\n\nThe sink shown returns data parsed directly from user-controlled YAML using yaml.UnsafeLoader, which disables the safety boundary intended for untrusted documents. For data interchange, parse only simple YAML types with yaml.safe_load so the loader accepts standard scalars, lists, and dictionaries without instantiating arbitrary classes.\n\nIf the application truly requires custom object deserialization, do not accept raw user-supplied YAML for that path. Instead, define an explicit schema, validate the parsed structure, and map allowed fields into application objects manually. This keeps deserialization behavior predictable and prevents attacker-controlled constructors or special tags from being processed.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nReplace yaml.load(..., Loader=yaml.UnsafeLoader) with yaml.safe_load(...) when parsing any untrusted YAML. Treat all externally supplied YAML as hostile, even if it appears to contain only simple values. After parsing, validate the resulting structure and types before using the data in application logic.\n\nIf only JSON-like content is expected, prefer a stricter format or enforce an allowlist of expected keys and value types. Avoid custom YAML constructors for request data. Where object creation is necessary, parse into basic data structures first and then manually instantiate approved classes from validated fields.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport yaml\n\ndef parse_user_yaml(user_input: str):\n    data = yaml.safe_load(user_input)\n    return data\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nPyYAML\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Unsafe Deserialization](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Unsafe_Deserialization)\n- [CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)\n- [PyYAML Documentation](https://pyyaml.org/wiki/PyYAMLDocumentation)\n- [Python PyYAML unsafe load vulnerability overview](https://sourcery.ai/vulnerabilities/python-pyyaml-unsafe-load-rce)\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/vulnpy/trigger/deserialization.py"
