          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "## Description\n\nUnsafe dynamic code evaluation. In Python, calling eval on data that is not fully trusted allows an attacker to execute arbitrary expressions in the current process. That can expose application data, trigger unexpected behavior, or lead to full code execution depending on the available scope and objects.\n\nThe sink shown here, return eval(code), evaluates the contents of code directly and returns the result. If code can be influenced by a user, file, network response, or other external input, the application is effectively treating data as executable Python. For cases where the intent is to parse simple Python literals such as strings, numbers, lists, dicts, booleans, or None, ast.literal_eval is the appropriate safer alternative because it only accepts literal structures instead of arbitrary expressions.\n\nReplace eval with ast.literal_eval when the input is expected to be a literal value. If the input is not meant to be Python syntax at all, use a format-specific parser such as json.loads for JSON data, or redesign the interface to pass structured values instead of executable text.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not execute untrusted input as Python code. Replace eval with a parser that matches the expected data format. For Python literal data, use ast.literal_eval. For JSON, use json.loads. If only a fixed set of operations is allowed, map explicit command names to functions instead of evaluating expressions.\n\nValidate and constrain input before parsing, and ensure the accepted grammar is as narrow as possible. Avoid fallbacks that retry with eval after parse errors. If dynamic behavior is required, isolate it behind a strict allowlist and keep attacker-controlled data out of the execution context.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n\ndef parse_code(code: str):\n    return ast.literal_eval(code)\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nimport ast\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-95: Improper Neutralization of Directives in Dynamically Evaluated Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/95.html)\n- [OWASP: Direct Dynamic Code Evaluation - Eval Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Direct_Dynamic_Code_Evaluation_Eval%20Injection)\n- [Python Documentation: ast.literal_eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n- [Python Documentation: eval](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\n"
          },
