          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "## Description\n\nDynamic code execution in Python can lead to code injection. Calling eval() on content that is built at runtime allows that content to be executed as Python code, which is dangerous when the value can be influenced by a caller, request data, files, environment variables, or other untrusted sources.\n\nIn this sink, return eval(code) executes the supplied expression directly and returns its result. An attacker can use this to run arbitrary Python expressions, access application data, or trigger further system-level actions through imported modules and object traversal. The safest fix is to remove eval() and replace it with a parser or explicit allowlist logic that only accepts the expected input format.\n\nIf the intent is to parse simple Python literal data such as strings, numbers, lists, or dictionaries, use ast.literal_eval() instead of eval(). If the intent is to support a limited set of operations, implement those operations explicitly rather than interpreting raw code.\n\nThis remediation is tailored to the shown sink by replacing direct evaluation with safe literal parsing and rejecting invalid input instead of executing it.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nDo not execute dynamically supplied Python code with eval() or exec(). Treat all externally influenced input as untrusted. Replace dynamic evaluation with structured parsing such as ast.literal_eval() for literals, json.loads() for JSON, or explicit dispatch tables for supported operations. When only a small set of values or actions is needed, validate against an allowlist and map inputs to known functions or constants. Reject unexpected input early and log failures without echoing dangerous content back to the caller.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n~~~python\nfrom ast import literal_eval\n\n\ndef evaluate_code(code: str):\n    try:\n        return literal_eval(code)\n    except (ValueError, SyntaxError) as exc:\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid literal input\") from exc\n~~~\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n~~~python\nfrom ast import literal_eval\n~~~\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)\n- [Python Documentation: eval()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#eval)\n- [Python Documentation: ast.literal_eval()](https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.literal_eval)\n"
          },
